<!--
    The ARM hard-float VFP contracts are members of the aggressive preset
    (options armfloatreturn and armfloatargs, stage P4 output-prototype and
    input-prototype; default off in the catalog). `auto` selects aggressive
    below 500 KiB, so on such a binary the preset is the default output.

    The script loads relocatable objects built from armfloat_preset.c and
    armfloat_preset_m4f.c (sources beside them, clang 14 -O2): armfloat_preset_hard.o states the VFP
    procedure-call standard (Tag_ABI_VFP_args=1), armfloat_preset_soft.o is
    the same source for the base standard (-mfloat-abi=soft).

    Pass 1 (hard, the preset with both options turned off) reproduces the
    defect: scale's double return is cut to its low word and its d0 parameter
    is lost, mix loses its double, and backfill's double arrives as two
    integer words. Pass 2 (hard, the preset) takes and returns each double
    whole, and backfill keeps the back-filled float in s1 beside the double in
    d1. In both passes copysign_bits, a float built from the bits of its two
    float inputs, keeps float parameters, and payload returns a quiet NaN with
    a payload as its bits rather than as NAN, which compiles to other bits.
    Passes 3 and 4 run the soft-float object the same two ways: the image
    states the base standard, so neither option fires and both passes print
    every value in core registers.

    The hard-float passes also pin what the preset must leave alone.
    scale_err converts a double to float on one exit and returns oflow's
    float through a tail call on the other, so it stays float. from_bits
    hands back an integer parameter in s0, which keeps its integer type
    rather than taking the float as a parameter its callers would convert by
    value. save_vfp stores the whole VFP bank (vstmia r0,{d0-d15}), which is
    no read of eight double parameters. from_bits_o0 is from_bits built
    without optimization, which spills its integer to the stack and reloads
    it into s0. set8f stores its eight float arguments with one vstmia
    r0,{s0-s7}, which is no bank save: they stay parameters. getpar is an int
    getter that loads a double field into d0 on one path and leaves d0 alone
    on the others; it keeps its int return. Passes 5 and 6 load
    armfloat_preset_m4f.o, a base-standard function a Cortex-M4F computes in
    through the AEABI helpers, with declared prototypes that put its double
    in d0: the call to __aeabi_dcmplt keeps its integer result in both
    passes instead of becoming the double left live in d0. Passes 7 and 8
    load armfloat_preset_m7.o (arm-none-eabi-gcc 10.3 -O2 for a Cortex-M7
    with double-precision VFP) with the options off and under the preset.
    neg_trunc widens its float into d0 to subtract it and returns the
    converted difference in r0, so it keeps its int return. to_ticks hands a
    double it computed in d7 to __aeabi_d2lz in r0:r1; the helper, declared
    `long long __aeabi_d2lz(double v)` as libgcc's debug information declares
    it, keeps the base standard, so to_ticks takes no double from d0.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x1000">1eff2fe1</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x1000" name="harness_stub"/>
